infographic compare-quadrant-quarter-simple-card
data
  title Four ways to order distributed writes
  desc The senior question is "does it detect concurrency?" — only one of these does it in software, and only one trusts the wall clock at all.
  compares
    - label Wall-clock LWW — keep the highest timestamp
      desc O(1), one number. Detects nothing: the fastest clock wins, so skew silently drops the genuinely newer write — and the client still gets 200 OK.
    - label Lamport timestamp — counter, max+1 on receive
      desc O(1), one counter. Gives a consistent total order but cannot detect concurrency: L(A) < L(B) does not mean A caused B, so concurrent writes get an arbitrary order.
    - label Vector clock — one counter per node
      desc O(n) metadata that grows with the cluster. Detects concurrency: flags two writes as concurrent so the store keeps both as siblings to merge — never silently lost.
    - label TrueTime — GPS + atomic clocks, commit-wait
      desc Real physical ordering. Bounds clock uncertainty to ε (about 1ms) and sleeps it out for external consistency — at the cost of special hardware and commit-wait latency.
theme
  palette #f43f5e #f59e0b #14b8a6 #6366f1
